  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Wednesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: \n\nSubtropical Ridge of high pressure shifting east allowing a upper\ntrough to move north into Utah to eventually get picked up by the\nwesterly flow over the northern tier of the US. This trough will \nact to set up another active day of convection for Nevada \nWednesday afternoon, although coverage in not expected to be as \ngreat as Tuesday. Models show a 20% to 60% chance for scattered to\nisolated showers and thunderstorms, with the eastern half of the \nstate seeing higher coverage. Once again storms that do form will \nbe capable of producing heavy rainfall, gusty outflow winds up to \n55 MPH, small hail, and localized flash flooding. By Thursday the \ntrough gets sent east, and in concert with the ridge shifting back\nwest, will allow for the monsoonal moisture flow to diminish \nthrough the weekend. As the ridge re-establishes over the SW US \nthe southern flow will shift to Southwesterly flow cutting of our \nmonsoon moisture supply as Thursday, Friday, and into the weekend \nsee shower and storm chances drop each day, eventually ending by \nMonday. The other side effect of the decreasing coverage of storms\nwill be the increase of temperatures that will return as high \npressure builds back over the area. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n \nThere is high confidence for scattered thunderstorms with heavy \nrainfall Tuesday and Wednesday afternoons.\n\nThere moderate confidence in the potential for localized flash \nflooding issues for northeastern Nevada for Wednesday afternoon\nand evening.\n\nThere is high confidence that storm chances and coverage will\ndiminish Thursday through Sunday as upper ridge cuts of moist\nmonsoonal flow.",
